# The best manual configuration of Muse Spark Open UD-Q4_K_XL on the Spark (GB10, unified memory; 2026-10-20, arm
# r6 of ~/ssdl/ab/chain-r6.sh; the GPU tier from one block and the host-memory margin of 4 GiB of the ledger's best
# run are the launcher's own on unified memory), as one SETTINGS file for run-ssd-llama.sh with models/muse-spark.conf:
#   SETTINGS=tools/adaptive/models/examples/muse-spark-gb10.settings MODEL_CONF=tools/adaptive/models/muse-spark.conf \
#     MODEL_DIR=<models> ./run-ssd-llama.sh
# The framework reaches it from zero, within noise (tools/adaptive/results-spark-20261021.md); it stays the
# reference.  Only values that differ from the launcher's defaults; the build is tools/adaptive/build.sh (NEON
# dotprod+i8mm, liburing).
# prefill through the ggml graph on the GPU: the CPU sink's grouped prefill is compute-bound on Muse (333 s to the
# first token at 4096 prompt tokens against 49 s); unaligned GGUF expert ranges are then read widened (derived).
# The threshold 0 still leaves the ubatches the GPU does not offload (below 32 tokens) to the sink.
GGML_KIMI_CPU_PREFILL_MAX_TOKENS=0
# the next-layer prefetch hint at depth 4: +7%/+11% decode over none; depth 8 was no better
GGML_KIMI_CPU_SINK_PREFETCH=4
# the context the benchmark turn needs (a 4221-token prompt and 256 tokens), not the model's 1M
CONTEXT=8192
# no checkpoint: the measured turn is one prompt (settings.py derives the same; one for a conversation)
CTX_CHECKPOINTS=0
# the measured arm ran cold without a disk prompt cache (a server keeps one: drop this line)
PROMPT_CACHE_DIR=
